= Robustness =

== Overview ==
'''Robustness''' is the ability of a system, model, network, or process to maintain specified structure or function under perturbations, faults, uncertainty, or variation.

A robustness claim is incomplete unless both the perturbation and the preserved function are identified.

== Perturbation-specific property ==
A system can be robust to one disturbance and fragile to another.

Examples include robustness to:

* random component failure;
* targeted attack;
* parameter variation;
* noise;
* environmental change;
* missing data;
* distribution shift.

There is no single universal robustness score.

== Robustness and resilience ==
Robustness often emphasizes maintaining function during perturbation.

Resilience often emphasizes recovery, reorganization, or restoration after disturbance.

The terms overlap in some fields but are not identical.

== Robustness and reliability ==
Reliability concerns probability of performing required function over time/conditions.

Robustness concerns insensitivity or maintained performance under specified perturbations.

A system can be reliable under normal conditions but not robust to unusual disturbances.

== Networks ==
In network science, robustness can concern persistence of connectivity or dynamics when nodes/edges fail.

Topology, interdependence, degree distribution, spatial structure, and dynamics can all affect network robustness.

